Research on Electric Vehicle Power Traction System: A Case Study of Traction Motor and Its Controller

Abstract

This paper focuses on the electric vehicle power traction system, with a particular emphasis on the key technologies of the traction motor and its controller. The article first briefly outlines the control indicators and requirements of the electrical traction system, and then analyzes the motor types, parameters, and selection criteria. Based on this, the paper provides a detailed explanation of the power circuit topology and control principles of the motor controller, accompanied by corresponding control block diagrams. Finally, the paper discusses the development direction and the main existing problems of the power traction system. The research shows that the optimized design of the power traction system is crucial for improving the performance of electric vehicles, and future efforts should focus on improving system efficiency and intelligent control.

Keywords: Electric Vehicle; Power Traction System; Traction Motor; Motor Controller; Power Circuit Topology; Control Strategy

Introduction

With the increasing awareness of environmental protection and the transformation of energy structures, electric vehicles, as representatives of clean energy transportation, are gradually replacing traditional fuel vehicles. The power traction system, as the core component of electric vehicles, directly affects the vehicle’s performance, economy, and comfort. The traction motor and its controller are key components of the power traction system, and in-depth research on them is essential for improving the overall performance of electric vehicles. This paper will explore aspects such as control indicators, motor selection, and controller design to provide a reference for the optimized design of electric vehicle power traction systems.

1. Control Indicators and Requirements of the Electrical Traction System

The control indicators and requirements of the electric vehicle power traction system mainly include efficiency, dynamic response, speed regulation range, torque control precision, and reliability. Efficiency is an important indicator for measuring energy conversion effectiveness, directly affecting the vehicle’s range. Dynamic response reflects the system’s ability to quickly respond to the driver’s commands, which is related to the vehicle’s acceleration performance and driving experience. The speed regulation range determines the vehicle’s maximum speed and climbing ability, typically requiring a range of 1:10 or higher.

Torque control precision affects the smoothness and comfort of the vehicle’s driving, generally requiring control within ±2%. Reliability is a basic requirement for ensuring the safe operation of the vehicle, including the system’s anti-interference capability, fault diagnosis, and protection functions. In addition, the power traction system must meet electromagnetic compatibility, noise, and vibration requirements to ensure the comfort and safety of the entire vehicle. These control indicators and requirements together form the goal system for the design and optimization of the power traction system.




