Inverter Configurations for Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motors (PMSM)

In the drive systems of Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motors (PMSMs), various inverter topologies can be employed depending on the system’s performance requirements and cost constraints. Among these, the most commonly used configurations are the half-bridge and full-bridge topologies.

Half-Bridge Inverter Configuration

The half-bridge configuration (as shown in Figure 3.2) is applicable exclusively to Y-connected (star-connected) PMSMs, where the ends of the three-phase windings are connected to a common neutral point. This neutral point is tied to a reference potential, typically the negative rail of the DC bus, forming a shared ground loop.

In this topology, each phase is switched using a single power switch. As a result, only three power switches are required, which significantly reduces cost and circuit complexity. However, the limitation of this configuration is that it only supports three-step commutation, meaning each switch conducts current for 120° of the electrical cycle. This causes relatively large current ripples and torque pulsations, making it suitable only for small BLDC motors in low-load applications.

