The three motor phases (A, B, C) are connected to the inverter outputs Ua, Ub, and Uc, respectively.

Figure 3.3 illustrates a full-bridge inverter using IGBT switches. Each phase is composed of an upper and lower transistor:
	•	V₁, V₃, V₅ are the high-side transistors, connected to the positive DC bus.
	•	V₂, V₄, V₆ are the low-side transistors, connected to the negative DC bus or ground reference.

These two transistors per phase operate in a complementary manner: when the high-side switch is turned ON, the low-side switch must be OFF to prevent short-circuiting the DC link.

To avoid shoot-through faults, a dead-time must be introduced between the turn-off of one transistor and the turn-on of its complementary counterpart, especially when using MOSFETs or IGBTs.

If both transistors are ON simultaneously while connected to the same potential, no current will flow due to the absence of a potential difference.

During motor operation, for instance, when V₁ and V₆ are both ON:
	1.	Current flows from the DC+ rail through V₁,
	2.	Enters phase A winding, exits through phase B,
	3.	Returns to the DC− rail via V₆.

This forms a complete current path and results in a segment of mechanical rotation.

In systems with high inductive loads, such as PMSMs, current continues to flow even after the switches are turned OFF. This phenomenon is known as freewheeling and occurs through the internal anti-parallel diodes of the switching devices.

To handle this, each IGBT typically includes a built-in flyback diode, which allows current to circulate safely during the off-state, preventing high voltage spikes and potential device damage.

