3.2 Control Circuit

The control circuit of a PMSM (Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motor) includes all components operating at a low voltage level. It mainly consists of the following parts:
	•	Central control unit (such as a CPU or processor)
	•	MOSFET/IGBT drivers
	•	Feedback connections (sensors and other control signals)

The CPU is responsible for processing feedback signals and sending them to the driver once processed, in order to control the switching of power devices. Since these signals operate at high frequencies, it is crucial to choose a CPU capable of handling high-frequency processing.

The role of the MOSFET/IGBT driver is to amplify the control signal output from the CPU (3.3–5V) and deliver it to the gate of the power device, enabling it to switch fully on.

This approach also has a major advantage: the power circuit is not directly connected to the sensitive control circuit, so even if a fault occurs, it will not damage the processor.

Figure 3.4 – Typical Application Circuit of the IR2101 Gate Driver [13]

The figure shows a typical usage of a MOSFET gate driver.

IR2101 Driver Explanation:
	•	Vcc: Power supply voltage, typically 12V or 24V DC
	•	HIN/LIN: Input control signals from the CPU, controlling the high-side and low-side MOSFET switches, respectively
	•	HO/LO: Output signals connected to the MOSFET gate pins for switching control
	•	Capacitor between Vb and Vs: Used to generate pulse voltage to ensure proper conduction of the high-side switching device

This driver typically adopts PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) control. By adjusting the ratio of on-time to off-time, the output voltage is regulated. This ratio is known as the duty cycle. The greater the duty cycle, the higher the output voltage.

To simulate a sine waveform, complementary switching pairs are used to adjust the duty cycle, thereby outputting a waveform close to a sine wave.

3.2.1 Control Algorithm

When controlling a Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motor (PMSM), rotor position information is needed in order to switch the power devices at the right time, ensuring smooth motor operation.

This positional information can be obtained with or without sensors. As long as the current rotor position is known, the semiconductor devices can be switched accordingly to achieve optimal rotational speed and torque output.

Control methods include:
	•	Sinusoidal control
	•	Trapezoidal control

Sinusoidal control is typically used in high-end equipment, offering smoother operation and suitability for high-power applications. Trapezoidal control, being simpler in structure, is suitable for scenarios with lower power requirements.

Sinusoidal control can be divided into:
	•	Scalar Control
	•	Vector Control

Scalar Control

Scalar control is commonly used in frequency converters for induction motors, but it can also be applied to PMSMs. Its basic principle is to keep the voltage-to-frequency ratio constant (U/f = const.), maintaining stable magnetic flux by increasing voltage and frequency simultaneously, thereby increasing rotational speed.

Advantages:
	•	Simple to implement
	•	Low cost

Disadvantages:
	•	Sensitive to load variations
	•	Cannot control motor output torque

