Vector Control

Vector control, although more complex, is the most widely used method in practice due to its high efficiency. The core idea is to measure the magnitudes of phase currents and voltages, and to compute current and voltage vectors based on the rotor’s magnetic flux position.

There are two primary types of vector control:
	1.	FOC – Field Oriented Control
	2.	DTC – Direct Torque Control

In FOC, stator current measurements are used to decompose the magnetic flux into two components:
	•	One from the stator current,
	•	One from the rotor’s magnetic field, typically measured via a position sensor (e.g., Hall sensor, resolver, or encoder).

The angle between these two flux vectors is called the control angle (Θ). By adjusting Θ, one can precisely regulate both motor speed and torque, independent of load variation.

