FOC vs. DTC

	•	FOC enables maximum torque production across the full speed range, even at zero speed.
	•	However, it requires complex algorithms and powerful processors.
	•	DTC, on the other hand, regulates the torque and magnetic flux directly.
	•	It does not rely on stator current vector transformation, but estimates motor state using rotor position and current.
	•	The resulting control signals are used to generate PWM signals for the inverter.

While DTC is structurally simpler and easier to implement than FOC, it generally causes greater current and torque ripples.

Trapezoidal Control

While the above control methods are mostly suited for motors with sinusoidal back-EMF, motors with trapezoidal back-EMF can be controlled using trapezoidal commutation methods.

In this work, trapezoidal control is chosen to reduce the complexity of the PMSM control system.

3.3 Principle of Operation of the Control Circuit

In this system, a trapezoidal control algorithm is used to drive the motor. Although it differs from sinusoidal control methods, the basic principles remain similar. The power stage consists of six transistors, whose gate signals are driven by gate drivers. In order to determine which windings should be energized at any given moment, the rotor position must be known.

Rotor position can be acquired via:
	•	Physical position sensors (e.g., Hall sensors), or
	•	Sensorless estimation using the stator voltage signals.

Sensorless Control Based on Back-EMF

To simplify the design, this system utilizes back electromotive force (Back-EMF) measurement as the main feedback method.

In a three-phase BLDC motor, only two phases are energized at any one time (e.g., Phase A is connected to the positive rail, and Phase B to the negative). The third phase is floating, and due to the changing magnetic field produced by rotor magnets, a voltage is induced—this is the back-EMF.

The amplitude of the back-EMF is directly proportional to the relative rotor position.
	•	When the rotor pole aligns with the stator winding center, the back-EMF reaches its peak.
	•	As the rotor continues rotating, the voltage crosses zero.
	•	These zero-crossing points are used as switching triggers for the commutation sequence.

