Six-Step Commutation Control

The six-step commutation method divides the full 360° electrical cycle into six 60° intervals, with each step lasting 120°. In each step:
	•	Two transistors are turned ON to create a current path,
	•	One phase is used as the floating phase for back-EMF detection.

The transistors are categorized into:
	•	T1, T2, T3 – High-side switches (connected to the DC+)
	•	T4, T5, T6 – Low-side switches (connected to the DC−)

